.\" UNIX V7 source code: see /COPYRIGHT or www.tuhs.org for details.
.TH QUOT 1M 
.SH NAME
quot \- summarize file system ownership
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B quot
[ option ] ...
[ filesystem ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Quot
prints the number of blocks in the named
.I filesystem
currently owned by each user.
If no 
.I filesystem
is named, a default name is assumed.
The following options are available:
.TP
.B \-n
Cause the pipeline
.B "ncheck filesystem | sort +0n | quot \-n filesystem
to produce a list of all files and their owners.
.B "Note:"
neither
.IR ncheck (1M)
nor
.IR sort (1)
is on this machine yet, so this mode has no producer for its input here; it reads whatever
standard input offers, in that format.
.TP
.B \-c
Print three columns giving file size in blocks, number of
files of that size, and cumulative total of blocks
in that size or smaller file.
.TP
.B \-f
Print count of number of files as well as space owned by each user.
.SH "BLOCKS ARE 1024 BYTES"
.B "Note:"
the block
.I quot
counts in is
.B 1024
bytes.
It is not this machine's filesystem block, which is
.B 3072
bytes \- 512 words of 48 bits \- and is three of them.
.PP
Two things follow.
Every number
.I quot
prints is a
.B multiple of three,
the smallest thing that can be allocated being one 3072-byte block: a one-byte file
costs three.
And the numbers are
.B half
what a PDP\-11 printed, v7 having counted in 512-byte blocks \- not a sixth, which is what
they were when this program counted in the filesystem block.
.PP
.B "Note:"
there is no
.I BSHIFT
on this machine and there cannot be, 3072 not being a power of two; the conversion is a
divide by the filesystem block and then a multiply, and both live in
.IR sys/param.h .
.PP
.IR Df (1M),
.IR du (1)
and
.IR ls (1)
report in the same block.
.SH "SUPER-USER ONLY"
.B "Note:"
.I quot
sweeps the raw disk's i\-list, and
.I /dev/rmd0
is mode 0600 because that one node is the contents of every file on the volume.
So this program works for the super-user and for nobody else.
It is deliberately
.B not
set-user-id.
.SH FILES
.I /dev/rmd0
\- the raw disk, and the default when no
.I filesystem
is named.
There is one EC\-5052 drive and there are no partitions.
.br
.I /etc/passwd
to get user names.
A uid with no entry there is reported as
.BI # n\fR.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B "cannot open"
names a device that could not be read;
.I quot
goes on to the next argument and exits 0 either way, which is v7's behaviour and is kept.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
ls(1), du(1), df(1M)
.SH BUGS
Holes in files are counted as if they actually occupied space.
.br
.B "Note:"
and, for the same reason, a file's indirect blocks are
.B not
counted: the figure comes from the inode's
.I size
rather than from its addresses.
.IR Icheck (1M)
would count what is really allocated, and is not ported.
.br
.B "Note:"
uids at or above 300 are not reported at all, and the totals are not cleared between
filesystems, so
.B "quot a b"
gives running sums.  Both are v7's, and neither can arise here: there is one filesystem.
